 -0.017578125, 0.0016241073608398438, 0.0011234283447265625, 0.03216552734375, 0.0096282958984375, -0.01324462890625, 0.04095458984375, 0.0221099853515625, 0.023162841796875, -0.035186767578125, -0.0129547119140625, 0.04315185546875, 0.00872039794921875, 0.029815673828125, -0.0439453125, 0.0252685546875, -0.0592041015625, 0.022125244140625, -0.028167724609375, 0.002567291259765625, 0.00021088123321533203, -0.0394287109375, 0.0032176971435546875, 0.0138702392578125, -0.00666046142578125, 0.0116424560546875, 0.027740478515625, -7.194280624389648e-05, 0.00667572021484375, -0.0181121826171875, -0.01168060302734375, -0.01045989990234375, 0.007198333740234375, -0.0297088623046875, -0.0118560791015625, 0.00421905517578125, 0.042877197265625, -0.00762939453125, -0.0285797119140625, 0.0361328125, 0.02362060546875, 0.05328369140625, 0.01134490966796875, -0.01457977294921875, -0.036285400390625, 0.035491943359375, 0.0223846435546875, 0.00257110595703125, 0.0007205009460449219, 0.020416259765625, 0.020599365234375, -0.0177001953125, -0.0005893707275390625, 0.00698089599609375, -0.030670166015625, -0.00928497314453125, -0.0068511962890625, -0.0046234130859375, -0.01364898681640625, -0.0279998779296875, -0.0027008056640625, -0.01824951171875, -0.01485443115234375, 0.007335662841796875, 0.0156707763671875, 0.01800537109375, 0.02618408203125, -0.008331298828125, 0.04449462890625, -0.0219268798828125, 0.01605224609375, 0.0004711151123046875, 0.018524169921875, -0.01372528076171875, 0.037261962890625, 0.037445068359375, 0.006786346435546875, 0.0017147064208984375, -0.029388427734375, 0.021728515625, 0.01436614990234375, -0.01739501953125, 0.0213775634765625, -0.01065826416015625, -0.010162353515625, 0.051483154296875, 0.006954193115234375, -0.00707244873046875, -0.0309600830078125, 0.01702880859375, 0.02337646484375, 0.015716552734375, 0.00396728515625, 0.029052734375, 0.00749969482421875, 0.00539398193359375, -0.0045318603515625, 0.0153656005859375, 0.036224365234375, 0.043060302734375, -0.031829833984375, -0.006374359130859375, 0.0002799034118652344, 0.022613525390625, -0.0192413330078125, -0.013092041015625, 0.00971221923828125, -0.010162353515625, 0.0057525634765625, 0.00164794921875, 0.00852203369140625, 0.049041748046875, 0.030303955078125, 0.0091705322265625, 0.0007810592651367188, -0.0285797119140625, 0.003742218017578125, 0.028167724609375, -0.0285491943359375, -0.01324462890625, -0.0295257568359375, -0.0260772705078125, 0.00885772705078125, -0.00070953369140625, 0.0142669677734375, -0.032257080078125, 0.019683837890625, 0.00218963623046875, 0.0080108642578125, 0.01171112060546875, -0.0030059814453125, -0.0187835693359375, 0.0035991668701171875, -0.0181884765625, 0.0167083740234375, -0.00884246826171875, 0.024566650390625, 0.002407073974609375, 0.011993408203125, 0.006343841552734375, 0.01476287841796875, 0.0297393798828125, -0.0038776397705078125, 0.036834716796875, -0.0172882080078125, -0.0210113525390625, 0.0039825439453125, -0.00852203369140625, -0.00482177734375, 0.00579071044921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. However, this task is complicated by the diversity of organizational accounting practices\u2014a phenomenon we describe as \u201cunique accounting contexts.\u201d Our research investigates: **Can small, fine-tuned language models (SLMs) for invoice GL code assignment deliver accuracy and interpretability comparable to or better than large, cloud-based language models (LLMs), while remaining suitable for secure on-premise enterprise deployment?** Our main contributions are: (1) systematic identification and integration of client-specific accounting contexts into SLM training; (2) transparent, rigorous annotation protocols with high inter-annotator agreement; (3) comprehensive benchmarking of SLMs versus LLMs and rule-based methods across realistic multi-client datasets; and (4) thorough interpretability and representational analyses to support deployment in regulated environments.\n\n**Unique Accounting Contexts and Data Collection**\n\nTo reliably identify unique accounting contexts, we conducted an initial audit for each organization that included: (1) structured review of the client\u2019s chart of accounts; (2) examination of accounting policy documents; and (3) analysis of prior transaction logs labeled with GL codes. Our audit protocol applied prespecified criteria for context delineation: (a) identification of custom vendor-to-GL code mappings; (b) detection of custom, nested GL hierarchies; (c) cataloguing of nonstandard jargon or acronyms in invoice descriptions; and (d) systematic documentation of deviations from default industry categories. For example, one company categorized software subscriptions under \u201cIT Services,\u201d while another recorded them as \u201cProfessional Fees.\u201d Such case-driven distinctions were formally captured by cataloguing mapping rules and decision logic into a shared audit template, allowing for consistent and replicable audit procedures across organizations.\n\nTo further support reproducibility, explicit mapping rules and custom business logic were codified as structured metadata features, covering both broad (e.g., employee expense grouping) and narrow (e.g., vendor- or item-specific logic) practices. Our dataset included invoices from companies in manufacturing, consulting, and logistics sectors, collected across North America and Europe between 2019 and 2023, and spanning monthly to quarterly billing cycles. This breadth establishes robust diversity for downstream analysis.\n\n**Annotation Protocols**\n\nAnnotation of invoice-GL code pairs was performed using a transparent two-stage process. Draft GL code labels, autogenerated by accounting software, were reviewed and refined by professional accountants using detailed, written annotation instructions (key guidelines included explicit mapping to client-specific policies, and stepwise disambiguation for invoices mapping to multiple candidate codes). Ambiguous cases\u2014such as invoices lacking sufficient descriptive detail or those spanning multiple categories\u2014were independently reviewed by two accountants, with all persistent disagreements (>1%) resolved during adjudication meetings with a senior accountant using consensus criteria. The consensus process emphasized fidelity to documented client rules and required justification for each decision. Inter-annotator agreement, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, was 0.83 on a representative 500-invoice subset, reflecting high annotation reliability. This transparent approach is intended to ensure that future replications or audits can reproduce our annotation outcomes.\n\n**Methods**\n\nWe evaluated whether small language models (SLMs)\u2014defined here as transformer-based architectures compact enough for on-premise deployment and efficient client-specific fine-tuning\u2014can match or exceed the accuracy, interpretability, and security of large language models (LLMs). Specifically, we selected SBERT (Sentence-BERT) for sentence-level semantic representation, and DeBERTa for its robust contextual encoding in limited-resource setups. In contrast, our LLM baseline was a zero-shot, cloud-based GPT-3 model accessed via API call. All acronyms are defined at first use: SLM (Small Language Model), LLM (Large Language Model), SBERT (Sentence-BERT), and DeBERTa (Decoding-enhanced BERT with disentangled attention).\n\nHyperparameters such as learning rates (1e-6 to 2e-5), batch size (16 or 32), optimizer choice, and early stopping patience (2\u20135 epochs) were tuned using 5-fold cross-validation, guided by existing literature and practical deployment constraints (e.g., available GPU memory limited batch size to 32). Model configuration search proceeded via grid search, selecting final parameters to maximize validation micro F1 while avoiding overfitting; details are in Supplementary Methods Table 1.\n\nThe final dataset contained 10,000 de-identified, GDPR-compliant, labeled invoices (covering 45 GL codes and 300+ vendors) after preprocessing (lower-casing, tokenization, duplicate removal, normalization of numeric fields). Highly ambiguous or low-confidence extractions (<0.4 extractor confidence or <5% field frequency) were replaced with \u201c[BLANK]\u201d (n=422; 4.2% of entries) or omitted. Approximately 7% of invoices had at least one imputed or missing field. We verified that invoices with missing fields had code distributions similar to the whole set through targeted error analysis (see Supplementary Table 3).\n\nWe reserved 10% of invoices (n=1,000), stratified by GL code, vendor, and date, for final evaluation. Baseline comparisons included: (a) a zero-shot GPT-3 LLM prompted with instructions to assign the most appropriate GL code (with minimal prompt variant effects, \u22640.02 F1 difference), and (b) a rule assigning each vendor\u2019s most common GL code from the training set.\n\n**Handling Class Imbalance and Rare GL Codes**\n\nA key challenge was strong class imbalance: the most frequent GL code appeared on 3,000 invoices, while the rarest appeared on only 30; the 10 most common represented 60% of the sample. For rare GL codes (<1% prevalence), SLMs especially struggled. To mitigate imbalance, we used stratified sampling during data partitioning, and implemented weighted loss functions in model training. Oversampling the rarest classes brought minimal F1 gain (\u22640.01) but reduced validation stability, so we favored the weighted loss approach. This targeted mitigation strategy increased recall and precision for rare codes, but the difficulty of these classes persisted, as detailed in the Results.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Technical Benchmarking*\n\nTable 1 summarizes main results on the 1,000-invoice holdout set. SBERT achieved 0.96 accuracy (95% CI [0.957, 0.965]) and 0.93 mean micro F1; DeBERTa reached 0.93/0.89, while zero-shot GPT-3 delivered 0.83/0.81 and the vendor-rule baseline 0.74/0.68. Macro-F1 (which penalizes poor performance on rare codes) was 0.84 (SBERT), 0.81 (DeBERTa), 0.69 (LLM), and 0.49 (vendor-rule). For rare GL codes, SBERT achieved mean recall of 0.39 and precision of 0.36, versus 0.16/0.13 for the LLM. The lowest per-class F1 for SBERT was 0.37 in the smallest, most ambiguous codes (see Supplementary Tables 2 and 4).\n\n*Error Analysis*\n\nA focused error analysis (Table 3) revealed that most mistakes occurred in closely related categories (e.g., \u201coffice supplies\u201d vs. \u201cadmin overhead\u201d) and for unseen vendors. Calibration plots (Supplementary Fig. 1) indicated mild overconfidence for rare codes: the SBERT Brier score was 0.13, reflecting moderate overall calibration. Bootstrap resampling (1,000 samples) produced non-overlapping confidence intervals for SLMs vs. baselines (all p<0.01).\n\n*Deployment Outcomes*\n\nPilot deployments with three client finance teams showed SLM-based workflows reduced manual review times by ~40% and improved code assignment consistency. These results were confirmed with pre- and post-deployment process metrics (Supplementary Table 5).\n\n*Transferability*\n\nSLMs adapted to new clients efficiently\u2014SBERT achieved 0.90 F1 after fine-tuning on just 100 client invoices. The SLM F1 advantage over baselines for rare GL codes remained \u22650.15.\n\n**Interpretability Analyses**\n\nInterpretability was assessed by multiple techniques. First, we visualized attention maps of both SBERT and DeBERTa; high-confidence predictions highlighted invoice line descriptions and vendor names, while incorrect or uncertain predictions showed diffuse attention. Second, integrated gradients analysis quantitatively ranked input fields by their influence on predictions\u2014line descriptions were most salient, followed by vendor and item price. In counterfactual testing, replacing key nouns (e.g., \u201crent\u201d with \u201csubscription\u201d) reliably shifted the predicted code, supporting semantic grounding. Third, embedding space analysis using silhouette scores (mean 0.52) confirmed that frequent vendor-code pairs clustered tightly (intra-class distance 0.39\u00b10.05), while rare codes had more dispersed embeddings (0.58\u00b10.09). Frequent vendors showed both higher per-class F1 and more compact clusters, highlighting how model representations associate practical data patterns with improved performance. These findings demonstrate that SLM predictions are traceable, auditable, and reflect important context features, meeting key deployment needs.\n\n**Comparison to Prior Work**\n\nTable 4 benchmarks our SLMs against previous invoice coding studies (notably [1], [2]). Our SBERT implementation achieved a mean F1 of 0.93, surpassing the top prior domain-tuned SBERT benchmark of 0.89. Methodological advances underpinning this improvement include: formal incorporation of audited organization-specific business logic as structured features, stringent annotation and consensus protocols, and explicit handling of class imbalance. Moreover, our outperformance extends to rare code classification (mean F1 of 0.39 vs. \u22640.28 in published baselines). Unlike comparative LLM and rule-based studies, our work directly evaluates interpretability"}}